apply a minimum height threshold to ensure every AIR has a baseline cost. The original contiguous chunking strategy actually produced reasonable balance, so maybe I'm overthinking this.\n\nWhat if I just do round-robin assignment on the sorted list? That's simpler than trying to perfect a cost model—I'd assign items alternately across threads, which naturally mixes large and small AIRs and keeps the counts nearly equal. With 305 items across 8 threads, each thread gets roughly 38 items, and the distribution becomes much more balanced.\n\nLooking back at the task description, it actually mentions \"interleaved (round-robin) assignment\"—that's exactly what I should be doing instead of struggling with LPT. Let me implement the round-robin approach since it aligns with what was originally suggested.\n\nI need to refine my cost model to account for both the height variance and the fixed overhead per AIR, so I'll combine them into a single cost metric.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3184,"cache_read_input_tokens":54112,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3184},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a7d8193f-9a6a-449b-9cda-d3b0ae271652","uuid":"cec9c1d6-ab31-472c-961b-9dfbb140633b"}
